# "The Ecology of Big Round Top" (William C. Darrah, Department of Biology, Gettysburg College; prepared for and published by the Eastern National Park and Monument Association in cooperation with Gettysburg National Military Park; the booklet is undated in the text, its rainfall table ends in 1967): read to the end by workstream bt-web, 2026-11-15 (15 pages).
# The booklet is natural history of the 1960s; only the statements below concern the land before July 1863 or name the fence rows of the valley floor. PDF p. 3 was looked at as a page image (digits read from the image). Its rights basis is not established by the document itself (see the rights note in INDEX.md).
@ NPS-BRT-ECOLOGY
%basis -
%images 3

3 | woodlots | Big Round Top's steep stony hillsides have been unsuited to any agriculture since white settlement | - | 1750-1780 to the 1960s (never farmed) | stated | The booklet says the steep stony hillsides of Big Round Top, from the time of white settlement between 1750 and 1780 to the present, have been unsuited for any kind of agriculture | #s6
3 | woodlots | The original virgin timber of Big Round Top (white pine, white oak, black oak, in places chestnut) was cut before 1850 | - | 1850 and earlier (the virgin timber was cut before 1850; the cover of July 1863 is second growth and is not described) | stated | The booklet says the original cover of virgin trees on Big Round Top, white pine, white oak, black oak and in places chestnut, was timbered before 1850 | #s7
9 | fields | The flat bottom land below Big Round Top, especially to the northeast, has been farmed for more than 150 years | - | pre-1863 to the 1960s (farmed for more than 150 years) | stated | The booklet says the flat bottom land below Big Round Top, especially to the northeast, has been farmed for more than 150 years | The flat bottom land below Big Round Top, especially to the northeast, has been farmed for more than 150 years.
9 | fences | Fence rows of the valley floor lined with red cedar (a 1960s description) | - | ~the 1960s (fence rows as the booklet describes them; not an 1863 state) | stated | The booklet says the fence rows of the valley floor below Big Round Top are lined with specimens of the red cedar | #s6
10 | fields | European weeds of the valley meadows were well established before 1800 (hedged) | - | 1800 and earlier (hedged: little doubt) | hedged | The booklet says there is no certain information on how early the European weeds reached the area but little doubt that many were well established before 1800 | there can be little doubt that many of them were well established before 1800
